Check that the image was built with the correct tag.

List images in the internal registry:
oc get imagestreams
Check the specific image stream:
oc describe imagestream custom-application
Verify the image tag:
oc get imagestreamtag custom-application:v1.2.3
Subtask 4.3: Verify Deployment Configuration
Confirm that the deployment uses the parameterized values.

Check the deployment:
oc get deployment custom-app-deployment -o yaml | grep image:
Verify the deployment status:
oc rollout status deployment/custom-app-deployment
Check pod specifications:
oc get pods -l app=custom-app-deployment -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].image}'
Task 5: Advanced Parameter Techniques
Subtask 5.1: Create Parameter Validation
Add parameter validation to ensure proper values are provided.

Create an enhanced pipeline with validation:
cat > validated-pipeline.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Pipeline
metadata:
  name: validated-parameterized-pipeline
spec:
  params:
    - name: image-tag
      type: string
      description: Tag for the container image (must follow semantic versioning)
      default: "v1.0.0"
    - name: environment
      type: string
      description: Target environment (dev, staging, prod)
      default: "dev"
    - name: replicas
      type: string
      description: Number of replicas (1-10)
      default: "1"
  tasks:
    - name: validate-parameters
      taskRef:
        name: openshift-client
        kind: ClusterTask
      params:
        - name: SCRIPT
          value: |
            echo "Validating parameters..."
            
            # Validate image tag format
            if [[ ! "$(params.image-tag)" =~ ^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]]; then
              echo "Error: image-tag must follow semantic versioning (e.g., v1.0.0)"
              exit 1
            fi
            
            # Validate environment
            if [[ ! "$(params.environment)" =~ ^(dev|staging|prod)$ ]]; then
              echo "Error: environment must be dev, staging, or prod"
              exit 1
            fi
            
            # Validate replicas
            if [[ $(params.replicas) -lt 1 || $(params.replicas) -gt 10 ]]; then
              echo "Error: replicas must be between 1 and 10"
              exit 1
            fi
            
            echo "All parameters are valid!"
    - name: display-config
      taskRef:
        name: openshift-client
        kind: ClusterTask
      runAfter:
        - validate-parameters
      params:
        - name: SCRIPT
          value: |
            echo "Pipeline Configuration:"
            echo "Image Tag: $(params.image-tag)"
            echo "Environment: $(params.environment)"
            echo "Replicas: $(params.replicas)"
EOF
Apply the validated pipeline:
oc apply -f validated-pipeline.yaml
Subtask 5.2: Test Parameter Validation
Test the validation with both valid and invalid parameters.

Test with valid parameters:
tkn pipeline start validated-parameterized-pipeline \
  --param image-tag="v2.1.0" \
  --param environment="staging" \
  --param replicas="3" \
  --showlog
Test with invalid parameters:
tkn pipeline start validated-parameterized-pipeline \
  --param image-tag="invalid-tag" \
  --param environment="testing" \
  --param replicas="15" \
  --showlog
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Parameter Not Found Error
Problem: Pipeline fails with "parameter not found" error.

Solution:

# Check parameter names in pipeline definition
tkn pipeline describe your-pipeline-name | grep -A 20 "Params"

# Ensure parameter names match exactly (case-sensitive)
# Verify parameter is defined in pipeline spec
Issue 2: Default Values Not Applied
Problem: Pipeline uses empty values instead of defaults.

Solution:

# Check if parameter has proper default value
oc get pipeline your-pipeline-name -o yaml | grep -A 5 "params:"

# Ensure default values are properly formatted in YAML
Issue 3: Workspace Binding Issues
Problem: Pipeline fails due to workspace binding errors.

Solution:

# Verify PVC exists
oc get pvc pipeline-workspace-pvc

# Check workspace names match between pipeline and pipelinerun
# Ensure proper permissions on workspace volumes
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

• Parameterized a Tekton pipeline by adding configurable parameters for image tags, repository URLs, and deployment configurations • Modified pipeline definitions to accept dynamic input values while maintaining default fallbacks • Executed pipelines with custom parameters using both YAML manifests and CLI commands • Validated parameter usage by examining pipeline runs and verifying that custom values were correctly applied • Implemented parameter validation to ensure data integrity and prevent common configuration errors

Why This Matters: Pipeline parameterization is crucial for creating reusable, flexible CI/CD workflows. Instead of maintaining multiple similar pipelines for different environments or applications, you can use a single parameterized pipeline that adapts to various scenarios. This approach reduces maintenance overhead, improves consistency, and enables teams to standardize their deployment processes while maintaining the flexibility to customize behavior when needed.

The skills you've learned enable you to build production-ready pipelines that can handle multiple environments, different application versions, and varying deployment requirements - all essential capabilities for modern DevOps practices in enterprise environments.
